title: Opposition Day Debate
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: commons
view-if: opposition_day_timer <= 0
on-arrival: opposition_day_timer = 3; month_actions += 1

= Opposition Day Debate

The Opposition chooses the subject for today's debate. The government will win the vote, but the point is the argument.

- @prices: The cost of living.
- @unions: The power of the unions.
- @taxes: The tax burden.
- @jobs: Unemployment.
- @easy_discard

@prices
on-arrival: labour_credibility -= 3 if inflation >= 9; labour_credibility -= 1 if inflation < 9; economic_liberalism += 1; thatcher_rating += 1

[? if inflation >= 9 : Prices are still rising fast, and Denis Healey has a hard afternoon. ?][? if inflation < 9 : Healey points out that inflation has halved under Labour. It's a fair point, and an uncomfortable one. ?]

@unions
on-arrival: union_backlash += 3 if strike_wave >= 4; union_backlash += 1 if strike_wave < 4; labour_credibility -= 2 if strike_wave >= 4; wet_dissent += 1

[? if strike_wave >= 4 : Ministers squirm. They can't defend the strikes, and they can't attack the unions that fund their party. ?][? if strike_wave < 4 : Ministers accuse us of wanting a confrontation that nobody else is looking for. ?]

@taxes
on-arrival: c1_con += 2*(1-dissent); ab_con += 1*(1-dissent)

Our speakers contrast British tax rates with West Germany's and ask why a skilled man takes home so little of a pay rise.

@jobs
on-arrival: labour_credibility -= 2; de_con += 1*(1-dissent); c2_con += 1*(1-dissent); economic_liberalism -= 1

Unemployment has doubled since Labour took office. The attack hurts the government, though the dries grumble that we sound like Keynesians ourselves.
